{"reference":"Ezekiel 18","book":"Ezekiel","chapter":18,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Individual Responsibility","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter rejects the sour-grapes proverb as a fatalistic way of explaining Israel’s condition. Its repeated contrasts between fathers and sons, righteous and wicked people, and those who turn or do not turn make the point that each person is accountable for his or her own conduct. The call to turn and live therefore treats a person’s present response as meaningful rather than as fixed by another generation’s wrongdoing.","verse_refs":["Ezekiel 18:1-32"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ezekiel_018_0d3c702b0b374ebbfaba92fa"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}